course: everyday
lesson: 112
title: Sharp things, small objects and little ones
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Sharp things are tools: scissors, needles and blades do their job because they cut and poke, so the same edge that cuts paper can cut a finger or an eye.
explain: Small children are explorers who put everything in their mouths and cannot judge danger, and their windpipe is only about as wide as a drinking straw.
explain: A good rule: anything that fits through a toilet roll tube is small enough to choke a child under three.
explain: For example, a girl in Texas finds a marble on the rug where her baby brother crawls; she picks it up and puts it high on a shelf before he can reach it.
explain: When a small child is really in danger, such as choking without a sound, the job of an older child is to shout for a grown-up and call the emergency number at once.
fact: Scissors are carried closed and pointing down and passed handle first.
fact: A needle or pin found on the floor should be put in a pincushion or given to a grown-up.
fact: Razors, craft knives and box cutters are for grown-ups only, because their blades are very sharp and slip easily.
fact: Staples and drawing pins on the floor should be picked up, because bare feet and paws step on them.
fact: A used needle found outside must never be touched, because it can carry dangerous infections.
fact: Babies and toddlers put things in their mouths and can choke on coins, marbles, Lego and balloon pieces.
fact: Anything that fits through a toilet roll tube is a choking risk for children under three.
fact: A child who is choking and cannot cough or cry needs a grown-up and the emergency number at once.
fact: The emergency number is 911 in the USA and Canada and 999 in the UK.
fact: Grapes should be cut into quarters lengthways for toddlers, and whole nuts and popcorn should not be given to small children.
fact: A toddler who is very quiet in another room is often getting into something dangerous.
fact: A baby can pull a tablecloth and bring hot drinks or heavy pots down onto himself.
fact: When a sibling takes your toys, use words, take turns or ask a grown-up, because hitting does not solve the problem.
exercise: How should you carry scissors across the classroom? | Closed, with the points facing down | Open, so you are ready to cut | Pointing forward to see where they go | Spinning on one finger
exercise: How do you pass scissors to a friend? | Handle first, with the blades closed | Blades first, so they can grab them | By throwing them gently | Open, so that they can start using them at once
exercise: You find a sewing pin on the carpet. What should you do? | Pick it up carefully and put it in a pincushion | Leave it there for a grown-up to find later when they step on it | Push it deeper into the carpet | Give it to the baby to hold
exercise: Why should you never play with a grown-up's razor? | Its blades are extremely sharp | It is too heavy for children | It only works on grown-up skin | It breaks if a child touches it
exercise: A parcel arrives taped up tightly. What is best? | Ask a grown-up to open it | Open it with a box cutter yourself | Cut it with a kitchen knife | Bite through the tape
exercise: Why should a drawing pin on the floor be picked up straight away? | Bare feet and paws can step on it | It may roll under the sofa | Pins rust if left on the floor | It could fall through the floorboards
exercise: You see a used syringe (a needle used for injections) in the park in Glasgow. What do you do? | Do not touch it and tell a grown-up | Pick it up carefully and put it in the nearest bin | Kick it into the bushes | Take it home to show your parents
exercise: Your baby brother picks up a coin from the floor. What should you do? | Take it from him gently and keep small things off the floor | Let him hold it, because coins are clean and too big to swallow | Laugh and let him put it in his mouth | Swap it for a smaller coin
exercise: How can you check if a toy part is too small for a toddler? | If it fits through a toilet roll tube, it is too small | If it is lighter than a spoon, it is fine for a toddler to have | If it is brightly coloured, it is fine | If it floats in water, it is safe
exercise: A toddler is choking and cannot cough or cry. What do you do? | Shout for a grown-up and call the emergency number at once | Give the toddler a drink of water | Wait for a minute or two to see if it passes by itself | Lay the toddler down for a nap
exercise: How should grapes be given to a toddler? | Cut into quarters lengthways | Whole, because they are soft | Frozen, so they last longer | Peeled but left whole
exercise: Why are whole nuts and popcorn not given to small children? | They can get stuck in a small child's airway | They are far too sweet and sugary for small children | They make small children sleepy | They stain small children's teeth
exercise: A balloon has popped at a party. What should you do with the pieces? | Pick them all up | Leave them for the toddlers to play with | Blow them up again into small balloons | Kick them under the table
exercise: Your little sister has been very quiet in the next room for a while. What is wise? | Go and check what she is doing | Enjoy the quiet and keep playing | Close the door so she stays there | Wait until she starts crying
exercise: You are looking after your little brother for a few minutes. What should you do? | Stay with him and keep him away from the kitchen, stairs and water | Leave him on his own and go to your room to play for a bit | Let him explore the kitchen alone | Take him to play by the pond
exercise: Why should hot tea not be put on a tablecloth near a baby? | He can pull the cloth and spill the tea on himself | The tea will stain the tablecloth | The baby might want to drink all of the tea himself | The tablecloth makes the tea go cold
exercise: Your sister keeps taking your toys. What is the best way to deal with it? | Use words, take turns or ask a grown-up to help | Hit her so she learns | Hide all your toys from her forever so she cannot find them | Take her toys in return
test: Which way of carrying scissors is safest if you trip? | Closed and pointing down | Open and pointing up | Closed and pointing forward | Open and held by one blade
test: Why do pins on the floor matter more in a house with a toddler or a puppy? | They might swallow them as well as step on them | Pins are only dangerous to grown-ups | Toddlers and puppies like the shiny colour and want to play with them | Pins grow sharper on carpets
test: Why should you not touch a needle you find in the street? | It can carry dangerous infections | It might be someone's lost property | It could be magnetic | Needles are too light to pick up
test: Which of these is a choking risk for a two-year-old? | A small rubber ball that fits through a toilet roll tube | A big soft teddy bear with sewn-on eyes | A large picture book | A wooden puzzle board with large chunky pieces
test: Ava's mum cuts grapes in four lengthways for her toddler. Why lengthways? | Round firm pieces can block a small airway, long thin ones are safer | It makes the grapes taste sweeter and juicier for little children | It stops the juice spilling | Grapes are easier to grow and pick that way, so they are cheaper in the shops
test: A boy in Ottawa hears his baby cousin go silent with a toy in her mouth, unable to breathe. What comes first? | Shout for a grown-up and call 911 | Look for the box the toy came in to read it | Ask her to spit it out later | Give her a biscuit to swallow
test: Why are box cutters dangerous for children? | Their thin blades slip easily and cut deeply | They only ever cut cardboard and nothing else at all | They are too blunt to be useful | They break if pushed too hard
test: A toddler is playing quietly under the kitchen sink. What could be happening? | He may be getting into something dangerous, so check | He is certainly safe in there because he is quiet | He is sleeping and should be left | Quiet toddlers never touch anything
test: Your brother grabs your favourite toy car. Which choice solves the problem best? | Ask him to give it back and offer to take turns | Push him over to get it | Break his toy as well so that he knows how it feels to lose one | Scream until he cries
test: found a needle on the path at the park, pick it up? | No, never touch it, tell an adult | Yes, pick it up and put it in the bin carefully | Yes, with your fingertips only | Kick it into the grass
test: grapes ok for the toddler? | Yes, cut into quarters lengthways | Yes, whole ones are fine if they chew properly | Only frozen whole ones | Never, toddlers cannot eat fruit
test: babys gone super quiet in the other room lol | Go and check now, he may be into something dangerous | Great, leave him, he's happy | Quiet means he's asleep for sure, so leave him alone | Wait an hour and then look
test: pass me the scissors | Hand them over closed, handle first | Throw them over gently | Pass them open, points first | Slide them over with the blades out
explain: A toddler in Ohio found a razor left on the edge of the bath and cut his hand; he needed stitches.
explain: A boy in Leeds left scissors on the floor; his baby sister crawled over and put them in her mouth and cut her lip.
fact: Babies and toddlers put everything in their mouths, so sharp things must be kept out of their reach.
fact: If a little child grabs something sharp, do not snatch it by the blade; calmly swap it for a toy and get an adult.
fact: Scissors, knives and razors are a common cause of cuts to small children at home.
test: left scissors on the floor, baby's crawling about | Put them away up high now | Leave them, she can't open them | Just close them | Put them under the rug
test: lil bro grabbed the razor, should i snatch it | Calmly ask an adult or swap it for a toy, don't grab the blade | Snatch it off him quickly by the blade before he cuts himself | Let him keep it | Chase him round the house
test: walking with scissors pointing up, all good | Carry them pointing down and closed | Pointing up is safer | Run with them so you get there faster | Wave them to show you have them
test: omg my aunt said her neighbour's baby swallowed a WHOLE lego man and it came out fine so lego's totally safe. real lesson? | Small toys can choke under-threes, so keep them out of reach | Lego is safe for babies to play with, because that one came out fine in the end | Only sharp things are a danger to a baby | Babies are safe if a grown-up is in the house
test: kids at the bus stop reckon a boy found a needle in the park, picked it up, n "turned into a zombie". what's just a rumour | The zombie bit; the real point is never touch a needle | That needles found outside can carry infections | That a used needle should never be touched | That you should tell a grown-up straight away about a needle in the park
test: Jack's mum left scissors out n his baby sis cut her lip, everyone says his mum's useless. pass it on? | No, it is unkind; just keep sharp things out of reach | Yes, people should know she is a bad mum who does not look after her kids | Yes, but only in the class group chat | Yes, it is true so it is fine to tell
exam: Identify the principal reason for which anything capable of passing through a toilet roll tube is to be kept from children under three years of age. | Such objects are small enough to choke them | Such objects are invariably sharp-edged and dangerous | Such objects are certain to be lost | Such objects are always made of glass
exam: Whereas scissors are to be conveyed from one person to another, state the manner prescribed for so doing. | Closed, pointing downward, and passed handle first | Open, pointing upward, and passed blade first | Closed, pointing at the recipient, and thrown gently across to them | Open, held by the blades, passed point first
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the preparation of grapes for consumption by a toddler. | They are cut into quarters lengthways | They are served whole, since they are soft | They are halved crossways across the middle | They are frozen solid before being served
exam: It is given that a small child is choking and is unable to cough or cry. Determine the appropriate course of action for an older child present. | Shout for an adult and call the emergency number | Offer the child a large glass of water to drink slowly | Wait several minutes to see whether it passes | Place the child in bed and allow it to sleep
